Since the electron moves through a potential difference, the gain in kinetic energy will be equal to the Potential energy lost by the electron.
The energy can be given by the formula $\mathrm{E}=\mathrm{VQ}$, where ' E ' represents potential energy in joules, and ' Q ' represents charge in coulombs.
$\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{E}=(1 \mathrm{kV})\left(1.6 \times 10^{-19} \mathrm{~J}\right) \\
& \mathrm{E}=1.6 \times 10^{-16} \mathrm{~J}
\end{aligned}$

The increase in the Volume of a body due to the increase in its temperature is called thermal expansion (Volume).
Coefficient of volume expansion is given by, $3 / 2 \times$ Coeff. of areal expansion
$\begin{aligned}
& =3 / 2 \times 1.6 \times 10^{-5} \mathrm{~K}^{-1} \\
& =2.4 \times 10^{-5} \mathrm{~K}^{-1}
\end{aligned}$

